\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Precedes Value Overestimation}
\label{sec:f2}

The factorial design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding as the strongest predictor. The \emph{primary} comparison---008d (trained, subsample ON) vs.\ 008f (no embedding, subsample ON)---differs only in embedding training, yielding $\nve = 4.12\times$ (eval 82.8) vs.\ $\nve \leq 0.97\times$ (eval 503.0). \emph{Secondary} comparisons against 008a/008c (unexposed embedding) co-vary subsample status and horizon alongside the embedding factor. The $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold (35K+ window) correctly classifies 27 of 30 experimental configurations across two environments (AUROC${=}0.953$, in-sample; LOEO cross-validated, Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}; Figure~\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}).
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_nve_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{NVE trajectories (mean $\pm$ seed range). Trained $\dt$-embedding (red, Walker Walk, $n{=}3$ seeds plotted; seed~4 data in Table~\ref{tab:complete_results}) exceeds the $1.5\times$ threshold (dashed) in all seeds; no-embedding control (blue) remains near $1.0\times$. Three trajectory types emerge: early collapse (seed~0), late-onset collapse (seed~3), and self-correction (seed~2). Dotted line marks the 35K diagnostic window onset.}
\label{fig:nve_trajectory}
\end{figure}

The trained $\dt$-embedding precedes CIVO in all five Walker Walk seeds (peak NVE $1.53$--$4.72\times$), universally degrading performance to below 30\% of baseline (IQM $175.2$, $n{=}5$; \citealt{agarwal2021precipice}). Combined with Cheetah Run (\S\ref{sec:f6}), CIVO onset is universal across all seven tested seeds ($n{=}7$, two environments) with 6/7 suffering sustained policy collapse, 1/7 self-correcting (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

%% ============================================================
%% F3--F5: Summary (details in Appendix B)
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F3--F5: Factorial Ablation (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial})}
\label{sec:f3f5_summary}

Trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure are \emph{jointly sufficient in tested configurations} for CIVO. Neither factor alone precedes pathological overestimation: trained embedding without multi-rate exposure (008a, 3 seeds, peak NVE $0.83$--$0.93\times$) and multi-rate exposure without embedding (008f, 3 seeds, peak NVE $\leq 1.41\times$) both remain below $1.5\times$. Cross-environment replication on Cheetah Run confirms generalization. Freezing the embedding (\texttt{requires\_grad=False}) dramatically reduces severity (NVE $0.19$--$1.88\times$ vs.\ trained $1.53$--$4.72\times$; 2/4 seeds below threshold, 2/4 transient self-correcting spikes; Table~\ref{tab:three_way_emb}); removing the embedding (008f) also avoids CIVO. Partial gradient blocking (exp\_044) does not prevent onset (peak NVE $4.45\times$) but permits delayed self-correction (final eval 538, 59\% baseline; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}), confirming that \emph{any} gradient pathway updating $e_{\dt}$ suffices to induce CIVO. Degradation scales monotonically with $\dt$ ($-47\%$ at $\dt{=}2$, $-81\%$ at $\dt{=}4$; Figure~\ref{fig:dt_amplification}).

\paragraph{ManiSkill manipulation tasks.}
None of three ManiSkill environments exhibit $\dt$-embedding-dependent CIVO. All value-ratio spikes ($4$--$11\times$) are environment-specific: frozen controls show comparable or higher peaks (Table~\ref{tab:cross_domain}), and all self-correct. CIVO is locomotion-specific in DreamerV3; the frozen-embedding diagnostic correctly identifies these transients as non-CIVO.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

World-model reinforcement learning enables sample-efficient learning directly on physical hardware \citep{wu2023daydreamer, hansen2024tdmpc2}. Real robotic tasks demand temporal abstraction: manipulation requires fast reactive grasping alongside slow approach planning, locomotion involves gait phases at different frequencies, and sim-to-real transfer must handle $\dt$ mismatches between simulation and hardware. TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm} shows that conditioning a world model on step size $\dt$ yields consistent gains within TD-MPC2's MPC framework \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2}. Can the same $\dt$-conditioning bring temporal abstraction to imagination-based actor-critic methods like DreamerV3 \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, where policies are trained entirely in imagined rollouts?

We find that it cannot. Every variable-rate configuration degrades performance catastrophically (14--19\% of baseline). Through a factorial analysis on Walker Walk \citep{tassa2018dmcontrol}, we isolate the combination of a \emph{trained} $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the strongest predictor. This combination enables the RSSM reward head to develop a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic value estimates, a failure mode we term \emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO), analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional} but model-induced rather than policy-class-induced. A normalized value-to-eval (NVE) threshold of $\geq 1.5\times$ achieves 27/30 classification ($n{=}30$, two environments, AUROC${=}0.953$, in-sample; LOEO cross-validated, Appendix~Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}).
Gradient decomposition reveals that gradient magnitude does not predict causal importance: the reward head contributes 38--49\% of $\nabla_\theta e_{\dt}$ yet reward-only stopping fails, while the observation decoder (16--22\%) carries the critical signal. A four-level intervention hierarchy (gradient, output, representation, architecture) shows that no tested intervention reliably eliminates CIVO across environments. In pure-imagination training, dual critics converge to identical value functions ($\rho > 0.999$), rendering Double Q pessimism ineffective; the min operation nonetheless enables CIVO recovery, while prevention requires min $+$ increased capacity. These findings are directly relevant to robotics: as world-model RL moves toward real systems with variable-rate sensing and multi-timescale control, understanding when temporal conditioning fails, and why standard countermeasures cannot fix it, is essential for safe deployment.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Failure mode identification.} We identify CIVO as specific to imagination-based actor-critic with temporal conditioning and isolate trained $\dt$-embedding $+$ multi-rate exposure as jointly sufficient in tested configurations (7/7 seeds, two locomotion environments, AUROC${=}0.953$, in-sample; LOEO cross-validated). Cross-environment validation spanning locomotion and manipulation (ManiSkill) establishes that CIVO is locomotion-specific; frozen-embedding controls on three manipulation tasks identify value-ratio transients as environment-driven, demonstrating the diagnostic's specificity on non-CIVO dynamics.
    \item \textbf{Architectural boundary.} $\dt$-conditioning succeeds in TD-MPC2's MPC re-planning but fails under imagination-based bootstrapping, where model errors compound into biased value targets with no real-data anchor.
    \item \textbf{Four-level intervention hierarchy.} Across gradient, output, representation, and architecture levels, no intervention reliably eliminates CIVO across environments. Gradient magnitude does not predict causal importance (reward head: 38--49\%, reward-only stopping fails). In pure-imagination training, dual critics converge ($\rho > 0.999$), eliminating Double Q pessimism, though the min operation enables recovery through a different mechanism.
    \item \textbf{Preliminary diagnostic.} A proof-of-concept NVE-based detector achieves TPR 95\% CI $[47.8\%, 100\%]$ ($n{=}5/5$), FPR $22\%$ ($2/9$), with predictive warning in 2/5 cases.
\end{enumerate}

