\subsection{Threshold Recommendation}

For the main diagnostic (Task~B2), we recommend $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ as the operating threshold for three reasons:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Maximum recall.} The $1.5\times$ threshold achieves 100\% recall, detecting all five CIVO cases including the transient case (009b, $\nve = 1.62\times$). Raising the threshold to $1.7\times$ loses 009b, dropping recall to 80.0\%.
\item \textbf{Best F1.} At $1.5\times$, F1 = 90.9\%, the highest among all tested thresholds for B2. The single false positive (008e\_v3\_s2) is mechanistically identifiable as IRD rather than CIVO.
\item \textbf{Sensitivity--specificity tradeoff.} Lower thresholds ($1.0\times$) add false positives without gaining true positives. Higher thresholds ($\geq 1.7\times$) sacrifice recall without eliminating the 008e\_v3\_s2 false positive (which requires $\tau > 3.78$).
\end{enumerate}

For Task~B1 (sustained CIVO only), perfect classification is achievable at $\nve \geq 3.9\times$, but with only $n_{\text{pos}} = 3$ the statistical power remains limited (accuracy 95\% CI: [71.5\%, 100\%]).

\subsection{Expanded ROC Analysis}
\label{sec:roc_analysis}

The original $n{=}16$ analysis (Tables~\ref{tab:sensitivity_b1}--\ref{tab:sensitivity_b2_full}) predates the SGS, RAVE, and TCN experiments. We extend the NVE diagnostic evaluation to $n{=}30$ configurations spanning all tested interventions. Figure~\ref{fig:nve_roc} shows the receiver operating characteristic curve with 95\% bootstrap confidence interval (10{,}000 resamples).

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\columnwidth]{../../artifacts/figures/nve_roc_curve.pdf}
\caption{NVE ROC curve ($n{=}30$ configurations, AUROC${=}0.953$, in-sample, post-hoc). The preset $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ operating point (star) achieves TPR${=}1.00$, FPR${=}0.18$. Three false positives arise from non-CIVO value overestimation: RAVE anchor pollution, obs-decoder SGS side effects, and IRD eval-driven collapse (008e\_v3 seed~2). Threshold calibrated and validated in-sample, post-hoc; cross-architecture validation deferred.}
% Note: AUROC computed on original n=29 validation set; exp_059 (s5) correctly classified under same threshold
\label{fig:nve_roc}
\end{figure}

We label a configuration as CIVO-positive if and only if (i)~it uses a trained $\dt$-embedding with multi-rate data exposure, and (ii)~NVE exceeds $1.5\times$ with co-occurring entropy collapse. Configurations exhibiting high NVE through other mechanisms (gradient truncation side effects, value anchor pollution) are labeled non-CIVO.

The three false positives (FPR${=}0.18$) are mechanistically distinct from CIVO: (1)~RAVE anchor pollution (exp\_028, the EMA anchor becomes inflated, producing overestimation through a different pathway); (2)~obs-decoder SGS-induced value distortion (exp\_025 seed~2, blocking observation decoder gradients damages the RSSM latent space); (3)~IRD eval-driven collapse (exp\_008e\_v3 seed~2, actor entropy \emph{increases} with evaluation collapse, mechanistically distinct from CIVO's entropy \emph{decrease}). All three produce NVE $> 1.5\times$ through non-CIVO mechanisms, indicating that the NVE diagnostic detects value overestimation broadly rather than CIVO specifically---a feature rather than a limitation for practitioners monitoring deployment health. The closest sub-threshold case (008f seed~4, NVE${=}1.41\times$) lacks $\dt$-conditioning entirely ($d{=}0$); its elevated NVE reflects transient value noise that self-corrects without the positive feedback loop characteristic of CIVO. The closest above-threshold positive (008d seed~3, NVE${=}1.53\times$) exhibits the full CIVO causal chain: trained $\dt$-embedding, entropy decrease, and evaluation collapse to 14\% of baseline.

