\subsection{F5: Imagination Horizon and Subsampling Are Secondary Factors}
\label{sec:f5}

Neither horizon length nor temporal subsampling precedes collapse without multi-rate data exposure: both trained-but-unexposed configurations (008a, 008c) maintain $\nve < 0.93\times$ (\S\ref{sec:f3}), while the trained-and-exposed counterpart (008d) collapses with $\nve = 4.12\times$.  The no-embedding configurations show cross-seed variance (008f: 503.0 vs.\ 009c: 148.3) but neither exhibits $\nve > 1.5\times$; low evaluation alone does not indicate CIVO.

The $3.2\times$ performance gap (008a: 777.7 vs.\ 008c: 240.9) quantifies the horizon effect independently of CIVO, as both conditions maintain healthy NVE ($\leq$0.93$\times$).

